      "label": "ii",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "If $n$ is not a multiple of 3 then $m=3p+1$ or $m=3p+2$ $(p\\in\\mathbb{N})$ giving $m^2=(3p+1)^2=9p^2+6p+1$ M1"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA14_June2021_Q9",
      "label": "ii",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "Or $m^2=(3p+2)^2=9p^2+12p+4=3(3p^2+4p+1)+1$ M1 A1"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "IAL2018_WMA14_June2021_Q9",
      "label": "ii",
      "kind": "working_line",
      "text": "$(3p+1)^2=9p^2+6p+1\\left(=3(3p^2+2p)+1\\right)$ is one more than a multiple of 3; $(3p+2)^2=9p^2+12p+4$ is not a multiple of 3 as 3 does not divide into 4 (exactly). Hence if $n$ is a multiple of 3 then $n^2$ is a multiple of 3 A1 (5)"
    }
